            pointsize: 0.04
            sizingmodel: perspective
            attensizenear: 4
            blending: true
            destblend: one
          position: [400, -400]
        - name: glsl_inhabitants_compute
          type: textDAT
          parameters:
            file: project1/container1/Visuals/inhabitants_geo/glsl_inhabitants_compute.glsl
            syncfile: true
            language: glsl
          flags:
          - viewer
          position: [325, -120]
          size: [130, 90]
          color: [0.9, 0.3, 0.4]
          tags:
          - glsl
          dock: glsl_inhabitants
        annotations:
        - name: annotate1
          mode: annotate
          title: Abstract Inhabitants (per detected object)
          text: |-
            Same POP+glslPOP-compute+pointsprite pattern as soul_geo/zones_geo. Samples registry/chopto_objects (32 slots: seedNorm/countNorm/alpha/roomNorm per row). Position is fully seed-deterministic (room->angular sector, seed->hash-based jitter) with only a small time-based drift/bob layered on top - identity from the seed, motion never replaces it. Color mixes a seed-derived hue with the live mood color. PointScale fades with the registry's alpha so objects dissolve on disappearance instead of vanishing.

            FIX NOTE: primitivePOP (points1) requires BOTH method=set AND keep=True to actually convert/strip the grid's original quad primitives to points - a fresh primitivePOP defaults to method=none (silently ignoring setprimtype) and keep=False, which left the original grid quad mesh rendering with corners scattered by the compute shader, producing a giant twisted/self-intersecting polygon. This was hard to diagnose because per-point P/Color/PointScale data was completely correct - the bug was purely in leftover primitive connectivity, not the data.
          position: [-220, -1060]
          size: [1290, 1490]
          color: [0.45, 0.45, 0.45]
